","To calculate the mass of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose of Ph-Foll Rinse, we use the given concentration (0.044% w/v) and the volume of the dose (10 mL). The concentration is given in percent weight per volume (w/v), which means 0.044% NaOH is 0.044 grams of sodium fluoride per 100 mL of solution.

1. **Calculate the mass of sodium fluoride in 100 mL of the solution:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of sodium fluoride} = \frac{0.044}{100} \times 100 \text{ g} = 0.044 \text{ g}
   \]

2. **Convert the mass to 10 mL of the solution:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of sodium fluoride in 10 mL} = \frac{0.044 \text{ g}}{100 \text{ mL}} \times 10 \text{ mL} = 0.0044 \text{ g}
   \]

3. **Convert the mass from grams to milligrams:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of sodium fluoride in 10 mL} = 0.0044 \text{ g} \times 1000 \text{ mg/g} = 4.4 \text{ mg}
   \]

Thus, there are 4.4 milligrams of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose of Ph-Foll Rinse.",fluency,"# Task Description
