1. Blood viscosity increases resistance to blood flow, reducing blood velocity. This is because the blood is more viscous, making it more resistant to motion and slowing the flow.

2. Blood viscosity increases blood pressure. This is because the increased resistance to blood flow requires higher pressure to maintain the same blood flow rate.

In summary, an increase in blood viscosity can lead to slower blood flow and higher blood pressure in the cardiovascular system. This can lead to various health issues, such as blood clots, blood clots, and other health issues.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

