To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following formula:

ΔS = Σ(n * ΔS°) - Σ(n * ΔS°)

where ΔS° is the standard entropy of the substance, and n is the stoichiometric coefficient of the substance in the reaction.

The reaction is:

3 CO(g) + O3(g) → 3 CO2(g)

We need the standard entropies (ΔS°) at 298 K for CO, CO2, and O3. From standard thermodynamic tables, we can find the following values (in J/mol·K):

ΔS°(CO) = 207.3 J/mol·K
ΔS°(CO2) = 238.1 J/mol·K
ΔS°(O3) = 205.1 J/mol·K

Now we can calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = (3 * ΔS°(CO2)) - (3 * ΔS°(CO)) - (O3*ΔS°(O3))

ΔS = (3 * 238.1 J/mol·K) - (3 * 207.3 J/mol·K) - (1 * 205.1 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 714.3 J/mol·K - 621.9 J/mol·K - 205.1 J/mol·K

ΔS = -112.7 J/mol·K

So, the change in entropy for the reaction is -112.7 J/mol·K.
