To calculate the lattice energy of CsCl, we can use the Born-Lefford equation:

Lattice Energy = (4πε * Z² * e² * α) / (4πε * ε₀ * r)

where:
- \( \epsilon = 8.998 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{C}^2 \text{N}^{-2} \) (vacuum permittivity)
- \( Z_1 = 2 \) (charge of the cation)
- \( Z_2 = -1 \) (charge of the anion)
- \( e = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \) (elementary charge)
- \( \alpha = 2.11 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J nm} \) (Born-Lande constant)
- \( r = 0.211 \, \text{nm} \) (distance between the ions)

Substituting the values:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{(4 \times 8.998 \times 10^{-12} \times 2^2 \times (1.602 \times 10^{-19})^2 \cdot 2.21 \times 10^{-19})}{4\pi \times 8.998 \times 10^{-12} \times 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \times 0.211}
\]

Simplifying the equation:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{(8.998 \times 2^2 \times 1.602^2 \times 2.21 \times 10^{-36})}{8.854 \times 10^{-22} \times 0.211}
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{(8.998 \times 4 \times 2.564 \times 10^{-36} \times 2.21 \times 10^{-19})}{1.8 \times 10^{-23}}
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{1.11 \times 10^{-49}}{1.8 \times 10^{-23}}
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = 6.17 \times 10^{26} \, \text{J}
\]

The lattice energy of CsCl is approximately \( 6.17 \times 10^9 \, \text{J mol}^{-1} \).
