CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpies of formation of CaO(s), H2O(l) and Ca(OH)2(s) are -635.1 kJ/mol, -285.8 kJ/mol, and -986.2 kJ/mol respectively.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the formula:

ΔH° = Σ ΔH°(products) - Σ ΔH°(reactants)

where ΔH° is the standard enthalpy of formation for each substance.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpy change (ΔH°) is calculated as:

ΔH° = ΔH°(Ca(OH)2) - ΔH°(CaO) - ΔH°(H2O)

Substituting the given values:

ΔH° = (-986.2 kJ/mol) - (-635.1 kJ/mol) - (-285.8 kJ/mol)

ΔH° = -986.2 + 635.1 + 285.8

ΔH° = -100.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide and water to form calcium hydroxide is -100.3 kJ/mol.
