Since $f(x)$ is an even function, $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all all $x$. Since $f(x)$ is increasing on $[0,\infty)$, it is decreasing on $(-\infty,0]$.

Since $f(x)$ is even and increasing on $[0,\infty)$, it is strictly increasing for $x \geq 0$. Thus, for $x \geq 0$, if $x_1 > x_2$, then $f(x_1) > f(x_2)$.

Since $f(x-1) > f(3-2x)$, we can find the range of $x$ that satisfies this inequality. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing for $x \geq 0$, the inequality holds if $x-1 > 3-2x$. Solving for $x$, we get:
\[
x-1 > 3-2x \implies 3x > 4 \implies x > \frac{4}{3}.
\]

Thus, the inequality holds for $x > \frac{4}{3}$. However, we must consider the domain of the functions $f(x-1)$ and $f(3-2x)$. For $f(x-1)$ to be defined, $x-1$ must be in the domain of $f$. For $f(3-2x)$ to be defined, $3-2x$ must be in the domain of $f$.

Since $f(x)$ is even and increasing on $[0,\infty)$, its domain is $\mathbb{R}$. Thus, $f(x-1)$ and $f(3-2x)$ are defined for all real $x$.

Since $f(x)$ is increasing on $[0,\infty)$, we have $f(x-1) > f(3-2x)$ if and only if $x-1 > 3-2x$. Solving for $x$, we get:
\[
x-1 > 3-2x \implies 3x > 4 \implies x > \frac{4}{3}.
\]
Thus, the range of values for $x$ is $\boxed{x > \frac{4}{3}}$.
