","To calculate the normal gravity at point P, we use the following formula:

\[
g_n = \frac{GM}{a^2} \left(1 - \frac{R^2}{a^2}\right)
\]

where:
- \(g_n\) is the normal gravity
- \(G = 6.6743 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \text{kg}^{-1} \text{s}^{-2}\) (gravitational constant)
- \(M = 5.9761 \times 10^{24} \, \text{kg}\) (Earth's mass)
- \(a = 6371.21 \, \text{km}\) (Earth's equatorial radius)
- \(R = 6362.121 \, \text{km}\) (distance from the center of the Earth to point P)
- \(a\) is converted to meters: \(a = 6371.21 \times 10^3 \, \text{m}\)
- \(R\) is converted to meters: \(R = 6362.121 \times 10^3 \, \text{m}\)

