The Dufort–Frankel method for the one-dimensional diffusion equation \(\frac{\partial \phi}{\partial t} = \kappa \frac{\partial^2 \phi}{\partial x^2}\) is given by:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Show that this method produces stable, non-growing solutions for any \(\Delta t\).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To show the stability of the Douford–Frankman method, we analyze the stability condition for the scheme:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \varphi_j^{n-1} + \varphi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Rearranging the equation:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Rearranging further:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2}} \left( \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_{j-1}^n \right) \right).
\]
This is a implicit time-time scheme, and the stability condition is:
\[
\frac{\Delta t}{\Delta x}^2 \leq \frac{1}{\kappa}.
\]
This is the same as the stability criterion for the Crank-Thomas method.

**Final Answer:**
The Diffusf-Friedman method is stable for all \(\Delta t\) if the condition \(\Delta t \leq \frac{\Delta x^2}{\sqrt{2\kappa}}\) is satisfied.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

