Consider a p-channel MOSFET with an oxide thickness of 500 Å and a donor doping concentration \(N_d = 10^{16} \text{ cm}^{-3}\). Calculate the body-to-source voltage (\(V_{BS}\)) needed to shift the threshold voltage (\(V_T\)) from its value at \(V_{BS} = 0\) by \(-1.0\) V.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the base-to-source voltage (\(V_{BS}\)) required to shift the threshold voltage (\(V_T\)) by \(-1.0\) V, we use the threshold voltage equation for a p-channel MOSFET:

\[
V_T = \frac{1}{2} V_{BS} - \frac{q \phi_s}{q_i} + \frac{q \phi_b}{kT}
\]

Here:
- \(V_T\) is the threshold voltage.
- \(V_{BS}\) is the base-tosource voltage.
- \(\phi_s\) is the built-work function of the substrate.
- \(\phi_b\) is the built-work function of the base.
- \(q\) is the elementary charge (\(1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C}\)).
- \(k\) is the Boltzmann constant (\(1.38 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K}\)).
- \(T\) is the temperature in Kelvin.

For a p-channel MOSFET, the threshold voltage is given by:

\[
V_{T} = \frac{1}{2} V_{BS} - \frac{1}{2} V_{BS(0)}
