An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} = 111.11 \text{ V/V}
\]
The gain \( A_v \) decreases by 6 dB due to aging. The new gain \( A_v' \) is:
\[
A_{vf,1} = 100 \left(1 - \frac{6}{10}\right) = 100 (0.4) = 40 \text{ V/V}
\]
The new closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_{vf,1}}{1 - m_v} = \frac{40}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{40}{0.9} = 44.44 \text{ V/V}
\]
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{44.44 - 111.11}{111.11} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-66.67}{111.11} \right) \times 100 = -60\%
\]

Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{-60\%}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
