<USER_REQUEST>
A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} \]  
The force on the proton is given by the electric field:  
\[ F = qE \]  
Substituting \( q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \) and \( E = 500 \, \text{N/C} \), we get:  
\[ F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} \]  
Now, substitute \( F = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} \) and \( m = 1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg} \) into \( a = \frac{F}{m} \):  
\[ a = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]  
The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) Using the kinematic equation \( v = v_0 + at \), where \( v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \), \( v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s} \), and \( a = 4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \), we solve for \( t \):  
\[ t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.25 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s} \]  
The time it takes for the proton to reach a speed of \( 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \) is \(\boxed{7.25 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}} \).
