\[
S = U^\dagger U,
\]

where \(U\) is the unitary matrix that relates the initial and final states:

\[
|f\rangle = U |i\rangle.
\]

The matrix elements of the \(S\)-matrix are:

\[
S_{mn} = \langle f_n | U^\dagger U |i_m \rangle = \langle f_n | S | i_m \rangle.
\]

The unitarity condition is:

\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Substituting the matrix elements of \(S\):

\[
\sum_l \langle f_n | S | i_l \rangle^* \langle f_n | S | i_n \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Using the definition of \(S\):

\[
\sum_l \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_l \rangle^* \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_n \rangle = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Using the orthogonality of the final states \(|f_n\rangle\):

\[
\sum_l \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_l \rangle^* \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_n \rangle = \sum_l \left( \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_l \rangle^* \right) \left( \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_n \rangle \right).
\]

Using the orthogonality of the final states and the orthogonality of the initial states, we can simplify the expression:

\[
\sum_l \left( \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_l \rangle^* \right) \left( \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_n \rangle \right) = \sum_l \left( \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_l \rangle^* \right) \left( \delta_{l, m} \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_m \rangle \right).
\]

This simplifies to:

\[
\sum_l \left( \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_l \rangle^* \right) \left( \delta_{l, m} \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_m \rangle \right) = \delta_{m, n} \sum_l \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_l \rangle^* \langle f_n | U^\dagger U | i_m \rangle.
\]

Thus, the unitarity condition simplifies to:

\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{nl} = \delta_{mn}.
\]

This is the unitarity relation for the \(S\)-matrix. 1. **Conclusion**: The unitarity condition is verified. \(\boxed{\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{nl} = \delta_{mn}}\).
